  >> DONE 48ca829. Confirmed on your box in its post-reset state: destinations [], no
     edges, yet the generator still emitted Screen 1 PGM + PRV. resolveMainScreenCount
     had Math.max(1, ...) on EVERY return path, so a main bus always existed even with
     nothing routed to it. You chose "reset means empty", so no destinations now means
     no channels - the config gets an empty <channels> block.
     Scoped carefully: configs that predate the destinations key keep the old floor
     (otherwise every legacy config loses its channels), and so does the
     "destinations exist but none are PGM/PRV" case - so an operator-GUI-only project
     like prrrr STILL gets a phantom PGM+PRV pair. That was option 3 in what I asked
     you and you did not pick it, so I left it. Say if you want it too.
     Seven gate tests broke. Six were fixtures that set screen_count without declaring
     any destination (the old pre-destinations style) - they now declare real cabled
     destinations so they still test what they are about.
     The seventh was a REAL conflict you should know about: a test asserted that an
     explicit always_on_top=false survives to the XML, which directly contradicts your
     new "any pgm port should have always on top true" rule from earlier today. It had
     only been passing because its fixture had no destinations, so nothing marked the
     port as PGM and the override never ran. I replaced it with two tests stating both
     halves: a CABLED pgm port forces true, an UNCABLED screen still keeps whatever you
     set by hand.
     NOT VERIFIED: how the running app behaves with zero channels. getChannelMap returns
     pgm: undefined in that state, and these are offline generator tests. So regenerate
     + apply on a truly blank box is untested - add a destination first, or tell me and
     I will go make the runtime side safe.


right here what to tests and commands to run via ssh on the test laptop to know why it lags so much.


================================================================================
LAPTOP LAG DIAGNOSTIC - run these over ssh on the test laptop
================================================================================
I ran every one of these on THIS box first, so the "healthy" numbers below are
real measurements from the working machine, not guesses. Run the same command on
the laptop and compare. Note that glxinfo / pidstat / sensors / iostat are NOT
installed on this image, so nothing here needs them.

  ssh casparcg@<laptop>

--- 1. The one-shot overview (start here) -------------------------------------
  curl -s localhost:4200/api/host-stats | python3 -m json.tool

  Look at: cpu.load1 vs cpu.cores, memory.freeBytes, gpu.text,
           processes.caspar and processes.highascg CPU%.
  HEALTHY HERE: load1 9.65 on 28 cores.
  RED FLAG: load1 at or above the core count = the box is saturated. On a
  4-core laptop, load1 > 4 is the whole story.

--- 2. Is the Node event loop actually starved? --------------------------------
This is the direct test of my theory. The laptop's caspar log showed ~450ms
between EVERY amcp command, including trivial ones, which is what a blocked
event loop looks like.

  node -e "
  let max=0,last=process.hrtime.bigint()
  const t=setInterval(()=>{const n=process.hrtime.bigint();const lag=Number(n-last)/1e6-50;last=n;if(lag>max)max=lag},50)
  setTimeout(()=>{clearInterval(t);console.log('max event-loop lag over 3s:',max.toFixed(1),'ms')},3000)"

  HEALTHY HERE: 0.2 ms.
  RED FLAG: anything over ~50ms. Hundreds of ms confirms starvation.
  NOTE this measures a FRESH node process, i.e. how starved the MACHINE is. It
  does not measure the highascg process itself - if this is clean but the app is
  still slow, the problem is inside the app, not the box.

--- 3. Time the AMCP round-trip directly ---------------------------------------
  node -e "
  const net=require('net'),s=net.connect(5250,'127.0.0.1')
  let n=0,t0;const times=[]
  s.on('connect',()=>{t0=Date.now();s.write('VERSION\r\n')})
  s.on('data',()=>{times.push(Date.now()-t0);if(++n>=5){s.end();console.log('AMCP VERSION round-trips (ms):',times.join(' '));process.exit(0)}t0=Date.now();s.write('VERSION\r\n')})
  s.on('error',e=>{console.log('AMCP connect failed:',e.message);process.exit(0)})
  setTimeout(()=>{console.log('timeout, got',times.join(' '));process.exit(0)},5000)"

  HEALTHY HERE: 1 0 0 0 2 ms.
  RED FLAG: tens or hundreds of ms. If THIS is fast but the app is still slow,
  Caspar is fine and the delay is on the highascg side.

--- 4. Pressure stall info (no tools needed, and very telling) ------------------
  cat /proc/pressure/cpu /proc/pressure/io /proc/pressure/memory

  HEALTHY HERE: cpu some avg10=4.26, full avg10=0.00 everywhere.
  RED FLAG: "full" above 0 on memory or io means real stalling - swapping or
  disk-bound. cpu "some" avg10 above ~30 means tasks are constantly waiting.

--- 5. Thermal throttling / CPU governor (laptops throttle, the tower does not) -
  cpupower frequency-info | head -20
  grep . /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/type /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/temp
  grep -c . /proc/cpuinfo

  Temps are millidegrees: 95000 = 95C = throttling hard.
  If the governor is "powersave" try: sudo cpupower frequency-set -g performance
  That alone can transform a laptop under sustained load.

--- 6. Who is actually eating the CPU ------------------------------------------
  top -b -n 2 -d 1 | head -30
  ps -eo pid,ppid,pcpu,pmem,comm --sort=-pcpu | head -15

  Expect casparcg at the top. If Xorg or firefox is above it, the problem is the
  GUI/compositor, not playout.

--- 7. Confirm which renderer Caspar got --------------------------------------
  grep -m1 "Initialized OpenGL" log/caspar_$(date +%F).log

  The laptop log said "Mesa 25.2.8 AMD" = integrated GPU, no NVIDIA. If it ever
  says llvmpipe or softpipe you are rendering 1080p50 ON THE CPU, which would
  explain everything on its own.

--- 8. Dropped/late frames --------------------------------------------------
  grep -ohiE "late frame|dropped|underflow|buffer.{0,12}full|too slow" log/caspar_$(date +%F).log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head

  Empty on this box. Anything here on the laptop means Caspar itself cannot keep
  up with the frame clock.

--- 9. Memory / swap ----------------------------------------------------------
  free -h; swapon --show

  Swapping on a playout box will produce exactly these symptoms.

--- What I already suspect (from your pasted log, unverified on the hardware) ---
The laptop is running TWO channels at 1080p5000 (50fps) with gpu_texture:false,
"frame copied to host for rendering", vsync on, on integrated AMD graphics. That
is a full CPU readback of a 1920x1080 frame 50 times a second, twice over.
Before anything else, try ONE channel at 1080p2500 and see if the lag vanishes.
Steps 2 and 3 will tell you whether it is the machine or the app.
